1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Presenting a technical decision. Decision, result, tradeoff, and the assumptions behind them

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,600
No query, no plan, no cost. Nothing to follow up on.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,700
Decision: what you chose, for which query.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,800
Result: what you observed on stated data.

5
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,300
Tradeoff: what it costs and what you will watch.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,200
Describe the observed plan change. Do not invent "3x faster" from one run.

7
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:34,200
There is no single correct database. The reasoning is what gets evaluated.

8
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,200
Choosing PostgreSQL does not by itself stop overlapping appointments. Test the rule.

9
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:43,100
"My data is JSON" decides nothing: PostgreSQL stores JSON, MongoDB can reference.

10
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:49,100
Who needs what, one relationship, one query result, one decision you tested.

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,100
Never depend on one live request. Keep a static fallback and label it.

12
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:59,000
Nothing secret goes on the projector. Present prepared, redacted results.

13
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,900
Unless your work established them, these claims come out.

14
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,400
Honest scope plus concrete reasoning beats inflated certainty.

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Remember: Decision → result → tradeoff, tied to one query and workload. Report what you observed; never invent a speedup. Design questions: ask, assume, choose simpler, name a test. Demo from prepared, redacted output with a non-live fallback. State scope: what the check covered and what it did not.

